  [1mModule layout[0m (no build step, native ES modules):

  index.html    shell + <main> mount point
  store.js      load / save / mutate tasks; owns localStorage
  reminders.js  pure functions: task + today -> status. No DOM, no storage.
  ui.js         render tasks to DOM, wire events
  main.js       wires the three together on DOMContentLoaded

  [1mTask shape:[0m

  {
    [36mid[39m:        string,   [32m// crypto.randomUUID()[39m
    [36mtitle[39m:     string,
    [36mdone[39m:      boolean,
    [36mdueOn[39m:     string | [34mnull[39m,  [32m// 'YYYY-MM-DD' — a calendar date, not a timestamp[39m
    [36mcreatedAt[39m: string    [32m// full ISO timestamp[39m
  }

  The one decision worth arguing here is [1m[38;5;153mdueOn[39m as a date string, not a timestamp.[0m "Due Thursday" is a calendar fact, not
  an instant in time. Storing it as a [38;5;153mDate[39m or epoch millis drags in timezone handling, DST, and the classic bug where a
  task due tomorrow shows as due today for anyone east of you. A [38;5;153m'YYYY-MM-DD'[39m string compared against today's local
  date sidesteps all of it with string comparison. The cost: you can't express "due at 3pm." I think that's the right
  trade for a task list; say so if you'd want times.

  [1mStorage:[0m one key, [38;5;153mtasks.v1[39m, holding [38;5;153m{ version: 1, tasks: [...] }[39m. The version field is three characters now and saves
  a migration headache the first time the shape changes.

  [1mReminder status is derived, never stored.[0m [38;5;153mreminders.js[39m computes [38;5;153moverdue | due-today | due-soon | none[39m from [38;5;153mdueOn[39m and
  today's date, fresh on every render. Persisting a status would be wrong within hours — midnight passes and every
  stored value is stale. This is also why the app re-derives on focus and on a timer while the tab is open, rather than
  trusting what it computed at load.

  Does this section look right — particularly the date-not-timestamp call?

